==Latin==
{{wikipedia|Latin profanity#Mentula and verpa: the penis|''Verpa''- Latin profanity}}

===Etymology===
Possibly borrowed from a {{etyl|gem|la}} language, from {{cog|gem-pro|*werpaną||to throw}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|verpa}}

===Noun===
{{la-noun|verpa|verpae|f|first}}

# {{lb|la|vulgar}} a [[penis]], a [[dick]]

''Verpa'' is also a basic Latin obscenity for "penis". It appears less frequently in Classical Latin, but it does appear in Catullus 47:

: ''vos Veraniolo meo et Fabullo<br />verpus praeposuit Priapus ille?''
:: ("Did that dick, that [[Priapus]], prefer you to my dear [[Veraniolus]] and [[Fabullus]]?")

''Verpus'', adjective and noun, referred to a man whose ''[[glans penis|glans]]'' was exposed, either by an [[erection]] or by [[circumcision]]; thus [[Juvenal]] has

: ''Quaesitum ad fontem solos deducere verpos''
:: ("To guide only the circumcised [i.e. Jews] to the fountain that they seek").

